  slide "Locust: distributed mode" {
    eyebrow: "Locust architecture"
    title: "Master/worker scales OS processes; greenlets stay cooperative inside each"
    lede: "A MasterRunner coordinates one or more WorkerRunner OS processes for horizontal scale, but the greenlet model doesn't change — each worker process still runs its own share of virtual users as cooperative greenlets."

    narration: "Locust does have real OS-process distribution: a MasterRunner process coordinates any number of WorkerRunner processes, each typically on its own machine or core. That's genuine horizontal scaling across processes. But the distribution boundary is coarse-grained — one process runs a batch of virtual users — and inside each worker process, those virtual users are still gevent greenlets sharing one cooperative scheduler, exactly as in single-process mode. The master does not synchronize individual request admission across workers; each worker's greenlets run independently once spawned."

    term: {
      word: "MasterRunner / WorkerRunner"
      meaning: "Locust's distributed-mode process roles. MasterRunner assigns a share of the target user count to each WorkerRunner process; each WorkerRunner then runs its assigned virtual users as independent greenlets with no further per-request coordination from the master."
    }

    points: [
      "MasterRunner distributes a user-count share to each WorkerRunner OS process.",
      "Distribution is coarse: process-level user counts, not per-request coordination.",
      "Inside each worker process, virtual users are still independent cooperative greenlets.",
      "Evidence: locust/runners.py — MasterRunner, WorkerRunner, DistributedRunner."
    ]

    caption: "Processes scale the greenlet pool; they don't synchronize individual requests."

    render: @scene {
